WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.888
SPEAKER: Our favorite
cat walking company

00:00:01.888 --> 00:00:03.720
has been using Cloud
DLP to automatically

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.065
redact and replace sensitive
values from their data.

00:00:07.065 --> 00:00:08.940
But what about data that
they need to protect

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.880
but still use for
things like billing?

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.670
Stick around and find out how
Cloud DLP's got them covered.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.500
Kitty Cat Walks has already
implemented Cloud DLP

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.990
to apply redaction and
replacement in their issue

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.150
ticketing system so
they don't inadvertently

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.117
log any sensitive information
from their customers.

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:33.950
Their billing department,
on the other hand,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.218
handles tons of
credit card numbers.

00:00:36.218 --> 00:00:37.760
They need to protect
that information

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.310
but also access those
values to process

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.480
payments for their premium
cat walking service.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.740
So while Cloud DLP
offers a bunch of options

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.590
to help you hide and remove
sensitive values in your data,

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.050
there's one technique
that leverages encryption

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.480
to de-identify
and, in some cases,

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.200
re-identify those
sensitive values.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.690
Crypto-based tokenization,
also referred

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.900
to as pseudonymization, is
a powerful de-identification

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.690
technique that uses encryption
keys to replace sensitive data

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.780
values with cryptographically
generated tokens.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.090
This method of
de-identification is especially

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.750
popular in industries like
finance and health care,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.730
where protecting data is a
matter of utmost importance,

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.910
but preserving data
utility is still desired.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.840
Cloud DLP supports three types
of cryptographic methods.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.810
First is deterministic
encryption.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.060
Here, the detected
data is replaced

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.340
with an encrypted
value and prepended

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.920
with an optional
surrogate annotation.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.020
This method supports
most input types

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.330
and is authenticated,
which makes it

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.670
the most recommended
tokenization solution.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.570
The next method is Format
Preserving Encryption, FPE.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.070
Like deterministic
encryption, FPE

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.590
will replace the value
of an encrypted string,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.420
except it's going to
be the same length

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.025
and use the same character
set as the original value.

00:01:57.025 --> 00:01:58.400
This is the way
to go if you need

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.980
to retain support for
any legacy data systems

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.160
that have strict length
character set requirements.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.520
The third method is
cryptographic caching.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.020
Here, DLP replaces sensitive
data with a hash value.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.600
Unlike the other two methods,
cryptographic caching

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.050
uses a one-way token so
it can't be reversed.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.600
It's the perfect solution if
you want each unique value

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.450
to be transformed into a
corresponding unique hash

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.080
value, but don't necessarily
want it to be reversible.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.500
More on that later.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.530
One huge factor
that sets all three

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.470
of these apart from most other
de-identification techniques

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.210
is that they allow
us to securely

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.260
de-identify data while retaining
its referential integrity.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.120
Referential integrity
allows records

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.250
to maintain the
relationship to one another,

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.580
even after being
individually identified.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.860
For example, if
we have this table

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.010
of data with cryptographic
hashing applied

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.650
to one of its fields using
the same cryptographic key,

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.650
each of the unique values will
have a consistent transformed

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.130
value.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.880
This means that data can be
de-identified for security

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.750
or compliance reasons, but still
used for business operations

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.020
and analytical workflows,
such as joining across tables

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.530
or aggregating data.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.800
So another powerful
feature of tokenization

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.960
is the ability to
reverse the encryption

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.390
to re-identify the data.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.850
Since deterministic encryption
and format preserving

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.920
encryption uses
symmetric encryption key

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.140
to tokenize data,
the same key can

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.500
be used to essentially
undo the de-identification

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.180
to reveal the original value.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.640
The option to re-identify
expanse utility of Cloud DLP

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.760
so that data can be
securely obscured

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.100
and readable only by
systems authorized

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.820
to access the encryption
key and the output value.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.280
Let's see how the DLP
API handles tokenization

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.150
by looking at this request
from a Node.js app.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.910
In this example, we're going
to apply format preserving

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.300
encryption to the credit card
number field of this sample CSV

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.560
data that out set
to a variable myCSV.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.060
This is the request
object that will

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.350
send to the de-identified
content function.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.720
First, we want to specify the
CSV data that we want to scan.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.880
In the de-identification
configuration,

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.360
we'll indicate a few things.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.092
Here, in the
transformations array,

00:04:12.092 --> 00:04:14.300
is where we indicate each
of the transformations we'd

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.980
like to apply.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.160
In this case, we just want to
target the info type credit

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.400
card number and provide an
FPE configuration for format

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.470
preserving encryption.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.450
There, we provide a
wrapped key, which

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.120
I've contained in the
variable myWrappedKey,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.870
and the Cloud KMS key we used
to wrap it in the form of a KMS

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:33.560
resource name.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.270
And I threw it into
the variable myKeyName.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.050
We'll also specify that we want
to transform our data using

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.870
a numeric alphabet and
provide a surrogate info type

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.130
string that will be
prepended to each value.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:47.690
The responses
function will include

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:49.700
the data we provided
in the request,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.740
except with the credit
card numbers replaced

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.220
with an encrypted string and
prepended with a surrogate info

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.310
type we specified.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.070
As you can see, each unique
value in our original data

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.070
remains unique after
transformation.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.400
With its referential integrity
intact, in a larger data set

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.890
we'd still be able to analyze
the data without accessing

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.335
the sensitive values.

00:05:11.335 --> 00:05:14.210
So how about re-indentification?

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.010
The re-indentified
content function

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.050
takes this request object.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.490
It looks similar to the
de-identified content request,

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.080
except we'll be passing
our de-identified data.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.900
As long as the surrogate info
type and the encryption key

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.810
we provide matches what we
use to tokenize our data,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.570
the result will be
our original data

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.310
with the sensitive
values revealed.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.250
Now that Kitty Cat Walks have
added crypto-based tokenization

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.400
to their DLP know-how, their
security and compliance teams

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.710
can sleep soundly knowing
that their customer billing

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.650
information is well-protected
on their systems.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.440
Next time, we'll take what
they've learned so far

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.080
and see how they'll use it
to construct DLP templates so

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.480
that they can reuse inspection
and de-identification

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.260
rules across different
systems and processes.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.330
See you soon.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

